      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To invoke a force majeure clause in a Singapore commercial lease agreement, the tenant must demonstrate that the interruption was beyond their control and that it was not reasonably foreseeable at the time of the lease agreement.\n\nThe tenant must provide written notice to the landlord within the specified timeframe, usually 30 days, to allow for the necessary arrangements to be made. This notice must specify the nature of the force majeure event and the impact it has had on the tenant's business.\n\nThe tenant must also provide evidence to support their claim, such as documentation from relevant authorities, government agencies, or other third-party sources. This evidence must demonstrate that the force majeure event was beyond the tenant's control and that it was not reasonably foreseeable.\n\nIn Singapore, the courts have established that a force majeure event must be one of the following: (i) war or armed conflict; (ii) invasion or attack; (iii) civil war or rebellion; (iv) riot or civil commotion; (v) natural disasters; (vi) fire or explosion; (vii) flood or storm; (viii) earthquake or volcanic eruption; (ix) government order or regulation; (x) act of terrorism; or (xi) any",
