      "content": "In Singapore, the courts typically interpret the \"unforeseeability\" aspect of force majeure clauses on a case-by-case basis, taking into account the specific circumstances of each situation. The courts consider whether the event was reasonably foreseeable at the time of the lease agreement, and whether the tenant had taken reasonable steps to mitigate the risk of such an event occurring.\n\nIn the case of a pandemic or a sudden economic collapse, the courts may consider factors such as:\n\n* Whether the tenant had knowledge of the risk of such an event occurring at the time of the lease agreement\n* Whether the tenant had taken reasonable steps to mitigate the risk of such an event occurring, such as by diversifying their business or maintaining a cash reserve\n* Whether the event was sudden and unexpected, or whether it was a gradual and anticipated development\n* Whether the tenant had a reasonable opportunity to prepare for the event, and whether they took steps to do so\n\nThe courts may also consider the language and scope of the force majeure clause itself, as well as any relevant industry standards or best practices. In general, the courts tend to favor a more nuanced approach, taking into account the specific circumstances of each case and the relative knowledge and foresight of the parties involved",
